THE RHODES ON THIS PAGE IS JEFF LEARMAN'S RECORDING OF HIS OWN PIANO
=====================================================================

The 65 files named `rhodes-<midi>-<layer>.m4a` in this directory are made from
jRhodes3d, a sample set by Jeff Learman.

    Author      Jeff Learman
    Instrument  a 1977 Rhodes Mark I Stage 73, his own, bought new in 1978 and
                recorded around 2006 directly from the harp connector
    Source      https://github.com/sfzinstruments/jlearman.jRhodes3d
                the `jRhodes3d-mono` directory, 65 FLAC files, 22,884,538 bytes
    Taken       2026-09-23
    Licence     Creative Commons Attribution NonCommercial ShareAlike 4.0
                https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/

positron.studio is a personal research site and nothing on it is sold, which is
what the NonCommercial term permits.


THESE ARE NOT HIS FILES
=======================

Every file here is a DERIVATIVE and none of them is what he published. What was
done to each one, by `demo/resources/fetch-jrhodes3d.mjs`, which is in this
repository and rebuilds all 65 from the source with one command:

    capped    at 4 seconds, rounded down to a whole AAC frame
    faded     out over the last 8 ms so a cut note does not click
    re-encoded from 44.1 kHz 16 bit mono FLAC to AAC in m4a at 96 kbps mono
    renamed   by MIDI note number and velocity layer

Nothing was equalised, compressed, normalised, pitch shifted or level adjusted.
The audio is his, shorter and through a codec.

SHARE ALIKE: these derivatives carry the same licence as the originals, CC
BY-NC-SA 4.0. Anyone taking them on from here takes them on under that.

`PROVENANCE-rhodes.json`, beside this file, records every source file, its byte
count, its duration, its peak, and the bytes and duration of what came out.


WHAT THE UPSTREAM LICENCE ACTUALLY SAYS, WHICH IS THREE THINGS
==============================================================

The label above is the strictest of the three statements the author's own files
make, because it is the only one that satisfies all of them. They are quoted
here in full rather than reconciled, so a reader can check.

1. The repository's own `LICENSE` file, in full:

    License for samples: CC BY-NC
    License for everything else: CC0
    License for musicians using this to make music: CC0

    Details:

    This sample set is provided free for use as a musical instrument.
    You can use the sounds in any way in your works as an artist,
    just as you could with a real instrument.

    To distribute the samples themselves, such as in an application, software
    instrument, or as a sample set, the jRhodes samples are licensed under
    CC BY-NC 4.0. To view a copy of this license, visit
    https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc/4.0 .

        BY: Credit must be given to the creator.
        NC: Only noncommercial use of the work is permitted.

    To use the samples in a commercial product, please contact me and I will be
    happy to grant a license.

    jjlearman@gmail.com

    Control files and example clips are in the public domain using CC0:
    "To the extent possible under law, Jeffrey Learman has waived all copyright
    and related or neighboring rights to jRhodes control files and example clips.
    This work is published from: United States."

2. The header of `jRhodes3d-mono.sfz`, the control file in the same repository,
   which drops the NonCommercial term and adds ShareAlike:

    // license: CC-SA-BY: Creative Commons Share Alike with Attribution
    // comment: Free to use in your music without share-alike or attribution

3. The index at `sfzinstruments.github.io/pianos/`, which lists this set as
   `CC-BY-NC-4.0` and the looped set jRhodes3c as `CC-BY-NC-SA-4.0`.

Serving the samples from a web page is distribution of the samples themselves,
which is the case his second paragraph names, so the artist grant in his first
paragraph is not the one that applies here and the NonCommercial term is.
Labelling these derivatives BY-NC-SA rather than BY-NC is a deliberate choice to
be stricter than the file requires under the reading that is most favourable to
this project, and it is what every one of the three statements above permits.

He invites a different licence in writing and has granted one before, a bespoke
CC BY-SA to the Strudel project. Nobody has written to him about this. His
address is in the licence he published.

`research/rhodes-packs-2026-09-23.md` sections 2 and 3 survey the whole question,
including a fourth statement in another of his repositories that reads CC0.


HIS OWN WORDS ABOUT THE RECORDING
=================================

From `README.md` in the same repository, because a visitor hearing this should
know what it is and what it is not:

    jRhodes3 is my sampling of my 1977 Rhodes Mark I Stage 73 electric piano,
    which I purchased new back in 1978. [...] It was recorded with EQ, with a
    treble boost and low-mid scoop, emphasizing the bell tones, and with
    substantial bark on the higher velocity layers.

    This is not intended to be the authentic original unprocessed Rhodes; it's
    intended to be what I wanted the Rhodes to sound like most of the time.

    I lost the original sample recordings in a home fire, but fortunately I did
    have distribution copies of the original looped and unlooped soundfont files,
    and these sample sets are recreated from them.
